      "note": "The immediate aim is to reproduce a known\nlanguage-model learning recipe. Math gives\nus an answer we can check. Recursive\nchoices come later. This is a small\nreproduction, not a new method or a\nreproduction of the paper's headline\nscores."
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "This example is invented. The model\ngenerates its own attempts. The checker\nsupplies only final-answer scores.\nRelative scores guide weight updates. We\nuse eight attempts per question. If all\neight scores agree, there is no within-\nquestion learning signal."
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Two runs started from the original\nweights: one chat-style, one question-\nonly. Each used the same 512 training\nquestions. We tested all three models in\nboth formats. All 500 test questions were\nexcluded from weight training; 64 were\nmonitored. No SFT stage was added."
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Across a row, test input stays fixed. Down\na column, weights stay fixed. Question-\nonly training gains nine over the stronger\nbase: 24 gains and 15 losses. It does not\nrepair chat performance comparably. One\nrun per format; small gains need\nreplication."
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Actual separate-test row 5, summarized. Of\nits 44 gains, 36 started as capped\nresponses. No generated code was executed.\nThis does not prove every gain is format\nrepair. We are checking repeatability\nbefore claiming a reliable gain or moving\nto recursive choices."
    }
